# Godot's import cache and editor state. Regenerated on open; never committed.
.godot/

# `*.import` files ARE committed, deliberately, and this line used to ignore
# them. They carry the per-file import settings -- and for
# assets/terrain/heightfield_overview.png those settings decide whether the
# RGB bytes survive at all. Height is encoded IN those bytes (R high, G low),
# so a VRAM-compressed import would silently corrupt the terrain while every
# checksum still passed. Losing the settings to .gitignore means the next
# clone re-imports with whatever the defaults are that week.
#
# `src/terrain/heightfield.gd` also asserts the loaded format at runtime, so
# the guarantee does not rest on a file being present.

# `.uid` files are NOT ignored, deliberately. Godot 4.4+ emits one per script
# and they are meant to be version-controlled: they are what keeps a reference
# stable when a file moves. They appeared here the first time the project was
# opened under 4.7, so this note exists to stop someone reading them as stray
# editor output and ignoring them.

# Export artefacts.
/export/
*.pck
*.zip

# Large sim artefacts fetched rather than committed. `assets/*/README.md`
# says which tool produces each and how it is pinned; the bytes are not this
# repo's to carry until the LFS-vs-fetch call is made (roadmap Phase 5 (e)).
# The terrain export COMMITS. At 6.0 MB -- a 1 km overview plus manifests --
# it is the "small overview + manifests" half of appendix (e)'s split, and
# committing it is what lets a fresh clone build the viewer from the repo
# alone. What does NOT commit is the full-resolution tile pyramid: 496 files
# and 141 MB, fetched by manifest per decision 948.
#
# THE CONTENTS ARE IGNORED AND THE DIRECTORY IS NOT, which is the difference
# between `tiles/` and `tiles/**`. Git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, and the pyramid's PIN lives inside the directory it
# describes -- as every other pin does. Excluding the directory would have
# taken the manifest with the bytes, which is the one file that must survive:
# a clone with no tiles is a working clone, and a clone with no pin cannot
# fetch them or even say what is missing.
/assets/terrain/tiles/**
!/assets/terrain/tiles/PIN
!/assets/terrain/tiles/.gdignore
# The three derived layers -- slope, aspect, distance-to-channel -- on the same
# grid as the pyramid, and 437 MB of it: three times the height tiles, because
# derivative fields are rougher and PNG compresses roughness badly. Same rule
# and the same shape of exception, and the README is kept too because it is
# what tells a reader with no bytes what the directory is for.
/assets/terrain/layers/**
!/assets/terrain/layers/PIN
!/assets/terrain/layers/.gdignore
!/assets/terrain/layers/README.md
# THE CLIENT FIXTURE NO LONGER COMMITS, and this paragraph said it did for
# four days after it stopped. `fce9bd8` moved it to fetch-by-manifest under
# decision 948 -- 28 MB against a 10,485,760-byte threshold -- and the line at
# the bottom of this file is what actually governs. What is committed is
# `fixture_client.json`, the manifest; the bytes arrive through
# tools/fetch_artefacts.py and nothing else.
#
# What it IS, unchanged: the quantised DISPLAY form -- band rows over the
# contract's declared bounds -- and not the fixture itself. The full-precision
# payload is 76 MB and stays in the simulation repo, because three of its rows
# are float64 for values below float32's floor and no colour ramp can show
# those. Anything needing the precision goes upstream.

.DS_Store

# Blender writes a .blend1 backup beside every save. The source is the .blend;
# the backup is the previous save of the same file and belongs to nobody.
*.blend1

# Screenshots from tools/screenshot.sh. Regenerable, and a frame is large;
# quote the measurement in a commit message rather than committing the picture.
shots/

# Fetched, not committed (decision 948). tools/fetch_artefacts.py brings these in;
# their sha256 lives in the neighbouring PIN and is authoritative.
assets/fixture/fixture_client.bin

# DA-6's compact dressing: the record the explorer's viewer draws (decision
# 2129, owner ruling 2130). FETCHED AND NEVER COMMITTED -- and NOT because of
# decision 948's size line, which these files are under: the largest is 7.3 MB
# and the four total 7.6 MB against a 10,485,760-byte threshold. 948 partitions
# how a large artefact is hosted; whether a public tree may carry a simulation
# artefact's CONTENT is decision 973's question, and the owner answered it
# "Fetched by PIN". Anyone who checks the sizes against the pyramid's reasoning
# will find it does not apply here, and this paragraph is why.
#
# SAME SHAPE OF EXCEPTION AS THE PYRAMID, AND THE LEVEL MATTERS. The pattern
# ends at the directory that HOLDS the files, not at `dressing/`, because
# `/assets/dressing/**` would exclude the `da6-compact` directory itself and
# git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded -- which
# would take the PIN out with the bytes, and a clone with no pin cannot fetch
# them or even say what is missing.
/assets/dressing/da6-compact/**
!/assets/dressing/da6-compact/PIN
!/assets/dressing/da6-compact/.gdignore

# §25 backlog 730's reference: the humans lane's own fold of DA-6 compact at the
# declared four years, and the renderer's palette (decision 2129, owner ruling
# 2130). FETCHED AND NEVER COMMITTED, on the same footing as the dressing's own
# bytes and for the same reason -- decision 973's question, not decision 948's
# size line, which these are under (largest 3.1 MB, six totalling 9.0 MB).
#
# A SEPARATE DIRECTORY AND A SEPARATE PIN FROM THE DRESSING, deliberately: this
# is the reference that judges the dressing, and a reference able to move with
# the thing it checks is a control that cannot fail. Same level rule as the
# dressing's and the pyramid's -- the pattern ends at the directory HOLDING the
# files, because excluding `dressing_renders/` itself would take the pin with
# the bytes and git cannot re-include a file whose parent is excluded.
/assets/dressing_renders/da6-compact/**
!/assets/dressing_renders/da6-compact/PIN
!/assets/dressing_renders/da6-compact/.gdignore

# The dressing's pin file: the rows the generator respected, and what the owner
# re-pins in the explorer (decision 2129, owner ruling 2130). FETCHED AND NEVER
# COMMITTED. At 2,077 bytes this is the clearest case that decision 948's size
# line is not the reason for any of these three fetches: it is five thousand
# times under the threshold. 948 says how a large artefact is hosted; decision
# 973 governs whether a public tree may carry a simulation artefact's CONTENT,
# and the owner answered that "Fetched by PIN".
#
# A THIRD directory and a THIRD pin, beside the dressing's and the reference's:
# this one is the EDIT TARGET, with a different lifecycle from either, and it is
# not part of `dressing_sha256`. Same level rule as the others -- the pattern
# ends at the directory holding the file, or the pin would go out with it.
/assets/dressing_pins/**
!/assets/dressing_pins/PIN
!/assets/dressing_pins/.gdignore
